# Mali-G52 (panfrost on fe400000.bifrost): keep the GPU runtime-resident.
#
# This works around a resume fault in this vendor 5.15 tree, found on the board
# after the panel went black roughly an hour into an idle session. In that state
# nothing above the GPU is broken: wayfire is alive and still page-flipping (the
# osd0 plane alternates between two framebuffers), the panel is powered with
# dpms=On and the backlight lit, chiron-privacy answers on D-Bus and ShowClock
# returns success, a freshly spawned client maps, and input is accepted. What
# comes out of the compositor is simply a zero frame -- a grim capture of the
# healthy session is 3.3 MB with all 256 byte values present, and of the failed
# one 6.8 kB with exactly one distinct value, 0.
#
# The GPU is what differs. In the failure state panfrost is runtime-suspended,
# its devfreq trans_stat shows no transitions at all, and at probe the driver
# logs `dev_pm_opp_set_regulators: no regulator (mali) found: -19` -- there is no
# mali supply in the DTS for the OPP layer to hold up across a power-off. No
# panfrost, MMU or job errors are logged either way, so nothing reports the
# failure: the shader cores come back without their state and every subsequent
# render resolves to black.
#
# Forbidding runtime suspend keeps the block powered and the fault never occurs;
# a soak of the same idle session with `power/control` set to on passed the hour
# mark with the clock still drawn. The cost is the GPU's idle draw on a
# wall-powered display, which is the cheaper side of this trade. The real fix is
# a mali regulator in the DTS so the OPP layer can restore the rail, and this
# rule goes away when that is proven.
#
# Both actions are matched on purpose. `add` fires before panfrost probes, and a
# forbid taken there is remembered when the driver enables runtime PM; `bind`
# fires after probe and covers the case where the driver re-allows it.
ACTION=="add|bind", SUBSYSTEM=="platform", KERNEL=="fe400000.bifrost", \
    ATTR{power/control}="on"
